This is a path traversal attack (also called directory traversal or dot-dot-slash attack).
What it means:
Breaking it down:

/ = root directory
./ = current directory
../ = parent directory (go up one level)
The attacker is trying to navigate to /etc/passwd (Linux password file)

The pattern /././.././etc/..password:

/. = stay in current directory
/./ = stay in current directory (redundant)
/../ = go up to parent directory
/etc/ = navigate to etc folder
/..password = this part looks malformed, likely meant to be /../passwd or just /passwd

What the attacker is trying to do:
Goal: Access the /etc/passwd file which contains:

User account information
Username list
User IDs
Home directories
(Old systems: password hashes, but modern systems store those in /etc/shadow)

How the attack works:

Web application takes user input for file paths
Attacker uses ../ to escape intended directory
Navigate to sensitive system files
